================================================================================================
(1) Cor 5.7 under an atomic input law
  (a) frozen noise: P[pi' is the unique lambda-MAP for all large n, every lambda] = P[x1 appears] * P[y(x1) = 0] = 1 * 1/2 = 0.5;
      pi' is non-equivalent: Hel_mu(pi') = mu(x1) * (1 - sqrt(1/2)) = 0.1464466094067262
  simulated 2000 runs of n = 4000: frozen  P[log2 Lambda_n(pi') = +1, pi' wins] = 0.505
                                   fresh   P[log2 Lambda_n(pi') > -inf]           = 0
  So Cor 5.7(c) is false for atomic mu with frozen noise; with per-query noise pi' dies at the first
  label 1 at x1. Non-atomic mu does not exist on a countable X.
================================================================================================
(2) Thm 3.2(d) with tau = sigma
  middle point (1,1): zero-window left steepness 1, right flatness 1; it is a (1,1)-kink.
  lambda= 1/2: MAP points [(2, Fraction(0, 1))]
  lambda=   1: MAP points [(0, Fraction(2, 1)), (1, Fraction(1, 1)), (2, Fraction(0, 1))]
  lambda= 3/2: MAP points [(0, Fraction(2, 1))]
  (1,1) is a MAP point only at lambda = 1, together with both ends: it is not a vertex.
================================================================================================
(3) After Prop 3.5: 'lambda-MAPs for lambda > 1 stay below l_U + (l_U+kappa0)/eps'
  (Dom) holds with kappa0 = 0: True; eps = 1, so the stated cap is 4.0
  lambda = 3/2: J(U) = 18.0, J(A) = 17.5; the lambda-MAP is A, of length 5 > 4
  correct cap (absorption): (lambda l_U + kappa0)/(lambda-1) = 6.0; it is below l_U + (l_U+kappa0)/eps only for lambda >= 1 + eps
================================================================================================
